1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,034
Two investment funds. Over the past year, both

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:06,304
returned exactly one percent a month on average.

3
00:00:06,404 --> 00:00:09,643
Same mean. Would you be indifferent between them?

4
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:13,284
Of course not. Fund A barely moves. Fund B swings from

5
00:00:13,384 --> 00:00:17,139
minus three to plus five. One number for the centre isn't

6
00:00:17,239 --> 00:00:20,317
enough; we need a second number for the spread.

7
00:00:21,416 --> 00:00:23,886
This lecture is about those two numbers: the

8
00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:26,631
mean, and the standard deviation. And about one

9
00:00:26,731 --> 00:00:29,492
surprisingly strong guarantee that connects them.

10
00:00:30,583 --> 00:00:33,558
Start with the mean. Put each observation on a line, as a

11
00:00:33,658 --> 00:00:37,118
small weight. Now look for the point where the line would balance.

12
00:00:37,216 --> 00:00:41,220
Put the pivot too far right, and the line tips left. Too far

13
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:45,940
left, and it tips right. There is exactly one balance point, and it's

14
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:50,386
the mean: the place where the deviations on each side cancel out.

15
00:00:51,483 --> 00:00:54,728
Now the spread. For each observation, measure how far it sits

16
00:00:54,828 --> 00:00:57,469
from the mean, and draw a square on that distance.

17
00:00:57,566 --> 00:01:01,894
The variance is, roughly, the average area of these squares. Far

18
00:01:01,994 --> 00:01:05,560
points get huge squares, so they count for a lot. The

19
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:10,195
standard deviation is the side of that average square, which brings

20
00:01:10,295 --> 00:01:13,793
us back to the units of the data: percent per month.

21
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:18,388
Why do we divide by n minus one instead of n? Here is

22
00:01:18,488 --> 00:01:22,878
the intuition. We want the spread around the true mean, mu. But we

23
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:27,504
don't know mu, so we measure distances from the sample mean instead.

24
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,466
The sample mean sits right in the middle of the sample. That's what

25
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:35,018
a mean does. So the data are always closer to their own mean

26
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:38,393
than to the true one, and the squares come out too small.

27
00:01:38,483 --> 00:01:42,895
Dividing by n minus one, a slightly smaller number, inflates the answer

28
00:01:42,995 --> 00:01:46,390
by just the right amount to make up for it, on average.

29
00:01:46,490 --> 00:01:51,157
With large samples the difference hardly matters. With small ones, it does.

30
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:57,087
Now the guarantee. Knowing only the mean and the standard deviation, how

31
00:01:57,187 --> 00:02:01,544
much of the data can lie far from the mean? Tchebysheff's theorem

32
00:02:01,644 --> 00:02:05,041
says: at most one over k squared of it can lie more

33
00:02:05,141 --> 00:02:09,704
than k standard deviations away. No matter what shape the data have.

34
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:13,800
Here's why. Think of the total squared distance as a fixed

35
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:18,394
budget. Every point that sits more than k standard deviations out

36
00:02:18,494 --> 00:02:22,424
spends at least k squared times s squared of that budget,

37
00:02:22,524 --> 00:02:26,029
all by itself. The budget runs out after one over k

38
00:02:26,129 --> 00:02:29,422
squared of the data. So far points must be rare.

39
00:02:29,516 --> 00:02:33,227
At two standard deviations, at most a quarter. At three, at

40
00:02:33,327 --> 00:02:38,007
most about eleven percent. For nicely mound-shaped data the true fractions

41
00:02:38,107 --> 00:02:41,366
are far smaller, about five percent and a third of a

42
00:02:41,466 --> 00:02:45,952
percent: that's the empirical rule. But Tchebysheff holds for anything.

43
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:51,467
So: the mean is where the data balance. The standard deviation is

44
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:56,470
the side of the average square. And Tchebysheff tells you that, whatever

45
00:02:56,570 --> 00:03:00,570
the data look like, being far from the mean is always rare.

46
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:05,157
Back to our two funds: same balance point, very different squares.

